  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Blue Origin has obligated $1B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date across 13 awards. The average award size is $78.6M and obligations are up 863.6% year over year. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration is the largest buyer, obligating $735M, followed by the Department of Defense with $287M. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing is the largest market, with $744M in obligations, and Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation accounts for $278M. Fiscal-year history shows a build to FY26 from earlier lows: FY21 obligations were $20K, FY22 were $357K, FY23 were $15.8M, FY24 were $10.6M, and FY25 were $106M. The current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity. Contracting activity concentrates on space vehicle manufacturing and related services, with a smaller amount labeled under research and development in the physical, engineering, and life sciences at $399K. This profile reflects a concentrated buyer base and large average award sizes in the current fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Blue Origin's top federal buyers?",
        "body": "The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $735M to Blue Origin. The Department of Defense obligated $287M to Blue Origin. Together those two agencies represent the bulk of FY2026 to date obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has Blue Origin's federal business changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are up 863.6% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations rose from $106M in FY25 to $1B in FY26. Prior-year obligations were $20K in FY21, $357K in FY22, $15.8M in FY23, and $10.6M in FY24."
      }
    ]
  },
